<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Our approach</p>
    <h1>Better decisions, followed by focused action.</h1>
    <p class="lead">Start with the business question. Understand the evidence. Choose a useful next step, then review what changed.</p>
    <a class="button" href="/free-marketing-audit/">Get a Free Marketing Audit</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">First, understand</p>
      <h2>Agree on the problem before prescribing the work.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>We begin with your offer, customers, goals, and constraints. A useful question might be “Why do mobile visitors leave before sending an enquiry?” or “Which search pages contribute to qualified leads?”</p>
      <p>Then we agree on what can be measured, what data is available, and who needs to act on the findings. This gives the work a clear purpose and an honest starting point.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The working cycle</p>
    <h2>Measure. Prioritize. Execute. Learn.</h2>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li>
        <h3>Measure the current position</h3>
        <p>Review data quality and map the path from discovery to a meaningful business outcome. Note gaps, definitions, and assumptions so everyone understands what the numbers represent.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Prioritize the opportunities</h3>
        <p>Compare potential impact, confidence in the evidence, effort, and dependencies. Choose a manageable set of actions rather than a list of everything that could change.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Execute with clear ownership</h3>
        <p>Agree on the scope, owner, success measure, and review point for each action. Coordinate analytics, search, social, and conversion work where the problem crosses channels.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Learn and adjust</h3>
        <p>Review what changed and what remains uncertain. Account for relevant context, record the finding, and use it to decide the next round of work.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Illustrative example — demo data</p>
    <h2>What a prioritized recommendation can look like.</h2>
    <p class="lead">This hypothetical travel website example shows the shape of an output. It describes no client project or achieved result.</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Observation</span>
        <h3>Mobile enquiry friction</h3>
        <p>In the demo dataset, 100 visitors begin the enquiry form and 30 complete it. A manual review also finds that the date field is difficult to use on a phone.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Priority</span>
        <h3>Fix and verify the form</h3>
        <p>First confirm that start and completion events work correctly. Fix the date field and review which fields are necessary before testing broader page changes.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Evaluation plan</span>
        <h3>Watch completion and quality</h3>
        <p>Compare form completion by device and check whether enquiries are relevant. Document traffic mix and seasonality; decide whether volume supports a controlled test.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p>A real recommendation includes the evidence source, data limitations, proposed owner, and effort estimate. Any estimate of potential impact is identified as an estimate.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Working together</p>
      <h2>Make the scope and next step clear.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Define the business outcome and deliverables before starting.</li>
        <li>Agree on access, implementation responsibilities, and review points.</li>
        <li>Separate observed facts, working hypotheses, and recommendations.</li>
        <li>Use reporting to support decisions about what happens next.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The free audit begins with your public website and stated goal. Any deeper use of private analytics, advertising, or customer data is agreed separately.</p>
